  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Tuesday)\nIssued at 222 PM CDT WED OCT 21 2026\n\nThis afternoon at the surface, a large area of high pressure is\npresent across much of the eastern half of Conus. In the upper\nlevels, northwest flow is present. Dry conditions will persist\nthrough the seven day forecast with warm days and cool nights. A\nsurface low will develop over the Northern Rockies Thursday into\nFriday and move northeast into Central Canada by Saturday. As it\ndoes so, the associated front will extend out to the south from\nit and push eastward out of the Rockies and into the Plains.\nAlso, upper ridging will start out over on the lee side of the\nRockies and traverse through the Plains into Eastern Conus by the\nend of the weekend. All that said, we will remain in a dry\npattern with little available moisture for cloud cover much less\nrain. We may have enough moisture for a little cloud cover by the\nbeginning of next week. By Monday, an upper shortwave moving\nnortheast out of the Western US will help to weaken the Eastern\nConus ridge yielding southwest upper flow and a return to some\ncloud cover. This is ahead of an approaching front by the middle\nof next week and the end of our extended forecast. Temperatures\nwill remain fairly similar over the next week with lows in the\n40s and highs in the 70s across Central Alabama.\n\n08",
